What we actually changed around the suspected race condition falls into
two separate groups.

The first three changes were primarily made to fix the original GUI
trashing / memory overwrite problem:

- In `library/ix_sigwinch.c`, we made handler installation idempotent
  by adding:

      if (u.u_idev_req) return;

  This prevents duplicate `IND_ADDHANDLER` calls and avoids losing or
  overwriting an already active request/handler.

- In `library/createextio.c`, we changed the initialization from only
  clearing `sizeof(struct IORequest)` to clearing the entire requested
  allocation:

      memset(ioReq, '\0', size);

  This was important because the SIGWINCH code uses an extended request,
  such as `IOStdReq`, and uninitialized data in the remaining part of
  the structure could cause unsafe behaviour.

- We removed the unconditional installation of the SIGWINCH handler
  before `main()`, first discussed around `_main.c` and later changed
  concretely in `library/ix_exec_entry.c`. The handler is now installed
  lazily from `library/kern_sig.c`, only when a program actually installs
  a real handler for `SIGWINCH`.

  This greatly reduces unnecessary `input.device` add/remove cycles.
  Programs that never use `SIGWINCH` no longer need an input handler at
  all.

Those changes addressed the original GUI trashing / memory overwrite
problem. They were not primarily race-condition fixes.

The later race-condition work focused on process teardown:

- We kept explicit cleanup through `__ix_remove_sigwinch()` during
  process teardown, including from `ix_close.c`, and made sure that
  handler removal was performed outside the `Forbid()` section.

  The teardown sequence was still essentially:

      IND_REMHANDLER
      CloseDevice()
      delete/free IO-request
      u.u_idev_req = 0

The important point is that we never proved that the race itself had
been completely eliminated. The teardown changes reduced the apparent
race window and made the failure much harder to reproduce.

A potential lifetime problem still remains because the handler keeps
references involving `is_Data = me`, `getuser(me)`, `u.u_idev_int` and
`u.u_window`. Correctness therefore still depends on `IND_REMHANDLER`
being fully completed before any handler-related data is released, and
on no input event reaching data that is already being torn down.

In short:

The duplicate/unnecessary installation and request-initialization fixes
addressed the original GUI trashing / memory overwrite problem. The
later teardown changes reduced the suspected SIGWINCH/input.device race,
but did not redesign the mechanism in a way that makes such a race
structurally impossible.

That is consistent with the freeze becoming much harder to trigger while
still potentially being reproducible with sufficiently precise timing.
